AC = \sqrt{AB^2 + BC^2} = \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{9 + 4} = \sqrt{13}
\] The height of the pyramid is the length of \(PA\). We can find \(PA\) using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle \(PAC\): \[
PC^2 = PA^2 + AC^2 \implies 5^2 = PA^2 + (\sqrt{13})^2 \implies 25 = PA^2 + 13 \implies PA^2 = 12 \implies PA = \sqrt{12} = 2\sqrt{3}
\] The volume of the pyramid \(PABCD\) is given by the formula: \[
\text{Volume} = \frac{1}{3} \times \text{Base Area} \times \text{Height}
\] The area of the base \(ABCD\) is \(AB \times BC = 3 \times 2 = 6\). So, the volume of the pyramid is: \[
\text{Volume} = \frac{1}{3} \times 6 \times 2\sqrt{3} = \frac{1}{3} \times 12\sqrt{3} = 4\sqrt{3}
